  I don't see how.  Do you think people will start 10-year games,
played in 1-hour blocks each December, solely in order to get lots
of presents from Santa?  Or that cheaters will set their clocks
back, the way they can now in order to get the luck bonus for the
full moon?
  Santa could work in exactly the same way as the "fake" mail
daemon -- just randomly pop in, deliver a "gift box," and pop out
again.  As with mail daemons, you would not have to worry too
much about Santa's being killed (although, as with mail daemons,
it might be possible).
  Santa's gifts could be "medium-level" presents -- nothing too
powerful, but nothing too banal either.  Books and clothes (read:
non-metallic armor items) are always popular Christmas presents
for the uninspired.  Tinning kits.  Magic whistles.  Other musical
instruments.  "Boring" rings and amulets.  Land mines. ;-)
  I would agree that Santa would be overpowered IF:

    * Santa were to deliver artifact gifts.
    * Santa were to deliver high-level scrolls or books.
    * Or high-level rings or amulets of lifesaving or suchlike.
    * If killing Santa were to give you more than trinkets.
    * If Santa were not unique.
    * If Santa could deliver more than one gift per game.

  And since someone needs to mention the obligatory nerdy
in-joke YANI that will never make it into the game: How about
Futurama's "Robo-Santa," who just wants to destroy you? :)


MORE ABOUT GIFT BOXES, and *another* YANI:

  Gift boxes (new item) would work like tins, in that once they
were opened, the packaging would disappear.  YANI: packages delivered
by the mail daemon.  (Those who play with "real" mail daemons
would never get packages, I guess.)

    "Aloha, Terence!  You've got mail!" --more--
    B - a gift box.
    Read what?
    You read, "To Terence, from The Lady"
    Apply what?
    You tear open the gift box.  It contains a spellbook! --more--
    C - a dark green spellbook.

  (Of course, there'd have to be some kind of abuse-preventer
here, too.  Maybe only deliver things that have already been
generated, but not yet seen, elsewhere in the dungeon?  Or use
the mail daemon to deliver gifts from the gods every so often,
as in the above scenario?)
  Cursed gift boxes (from bones levels) would, of course, contain
mail bombs.

  In keeping with my penchant for crazy-AI-requiring Nethack
stunts, it would be really cool to implement a whole dungeon-
wide mail system (using the mail daemon, naturally).

